    "\n",
    "### 4.7 Evaluate Forecast Accuracy (Optional)\n",
    "\n",
    "How do we know if our forecast is any good? In real projects, we split the data into a training set (to fit the model) and a test set (to check predictions). We'll use common metrics like MAE (mean absolute error) and RMSE (root mean squared error) to measure accuracy. Lower values mean better predictions!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 78,
   "id": "df976f8b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "MAE: 9.16\n",
      "RMSE: 12.18\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Example: Split into train/test and evaluate\n",
    "split = int(0.8 * len(series))\n",
    "train, test = series[:split], series[split:]\n",
    "model = ARIMA(train, order=(1, 1, 1))\n",
    "model_fit = model.fit()\n",
    "predictions = model_fit.forecast(steps=len(test))\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error\n",
